WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.101
Every grounded answer in this
series comes from one place.

2
00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:08.532
This episode: the
knowledge library — what goes in,

3
00:00:08.532 --> 00:00:12.030
how it becomes searchable,
and who gets to see what.

4
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.125
Documents live here.

5
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:18.861
Reports, guidelines,
meeting notes — drop them in,

6
00:00:18.861 --> 00:00:22.971
and the indexing pipeline
takes over: extract the text,

7
00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:26.857
split it into passages,
make every passage findable.

8
00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:30.070
The Indexed badge
means: ready to be cited.

9
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.364
One thing worth being precise
about: indexing is not training.

10
00:00:35.364 --> 00:00:37.411
Your documents change no model.

11
00:00:37.411 --> 00:00:42.627
They stay in your workspace, and at
answer time the agent retrieves exactly the

12
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:45.598
passages it needs —
with the source attached.

13
00:00:45.598 --> 00:00:49.230
That is why you could check
every claim in episode two.

14
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.429
For facts that live in nobody’s
document, there are knowledge entries — short,

15
00:00:56.429 --> 00:00:57.583
curated truths.

16
00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:01.505
We add one now: the
returns pilot for annual plans.

17
00:01:01.505 --> 00:01:07.350
Topic, content, save… and it is
already part of what every agent here knows.

18
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.218
Some knowledge is not prose at all.

19
00:01:11.218 --> 00:01:15.943
Products, customers, vendors —
typed records with real fields.

20
00:01:15.943 --> 00:01:21.888
An agent reads the price from the row
instead of guessing it from a paragraph.

21
00:01:21.888 --> 00:01:24.860
Documents for text, records for values.

22
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.941
Public websites join through the crawler.

23
00:01:28.941 --> 00:01:34.281
Hand it a domain and a rhythm, and it
keeps the index in step with the site.

24
00:01:34.281 --> 00:01:38.497
One honest boundary: it sees
what an anonymous visitor sees.

25
00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:42.220
Anything behind a login
belongs in documents instead.

26
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.905
And who sees what is a
decision, not an accident.

27
00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:52.736
An agent’s knowledge scope names exactly
what it may read — team documents stay in

28
00:01:52.736 --> 00:01:55.936
the team, workspace
documents serve everyone,

29
00:01:55.936 --> 00:01:57.998
uploads stay with their chat.

30
00:01:57.998 --> 00:02:01.910
Give each agent the smallest
library that does the job.

31
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.612
One habit keeps all of
this trustworthy: curate.

32
00:02:06.612 --> 00:02:12.230
The model believes what you give it — a
stale price list or a duplicate draft

33
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.638
becomes a confident wrong answer.

34
00:02:14.638 --> 00:02:18.651
Treat the library like a
wiki the whole company quotes.

35
00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:20.620
Because from now on, it is.

36
00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:22.581
The proof.

37
00:02:22.581 --> 00:02:28.901
We ask about the returns pilot we added
two minutes ago… and there it is — answered

38
00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:31.109
from the entry, source named.

39
00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.699
Knowledge in, grounded answer out.

40
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:43.088
That is the library: documents
indexed, facts pinned as entries,

41
00:02:43.088 --> 00:02:48.768
records typed, websites crawled —
and scopes deciding who reads what.

42
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:52.061
Next episode: building your first agent.

43
00:02:53.261 --> 00:02:58.337
The knowledge section of the
documentation covers every shape you saw here.

44
00:02:58.337 --> 00:02:59.961
See you in episode four.

